---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/static/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { href: '/', label: 'Home' },
  { href: '/privacy/', label: 'Privacy' },
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How the standalone Open Mermaid browser application stores, processes, shares, and exports diagram data."
  canonicalPath="/privacy/"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <div class="shell">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />
    <header class="page-hero page-hero--compact measure">
      <p class="eyebrow">Version 1 privacy notice</p>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Diagram content stays on this device unless you explicitly share or export it.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">Last updated: 2026-07-22</p>
    </header>

    <article class="measure policy-copy">
      <section>
        <h2>Plain-language summary</h2>
        <p>
          The standalone Open Mermaid application is delivered as static site files. It has no
          account system and no application server that receives your diagram source. It does not
          include a network analytics service, session replay, AI services, or ChatGPT connectivity
          in version 1.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Data stored in your browser</h2>
        <p>
          Open Mermaid uses IndexedDB to keep named documents, Mermaid source, appearance choices,
          revisions, and timestamps on the current browser profile. A single pending active draft
          may also be mirrored briefly in same-origin browser storage so closing a page cannot
          discard an edit before its asynchronous IndexedDB write starts. That recovery record is
          removed after the matching save. Theme and interface preferences may use browser storage.
          This data is governed by the device, browser profile, private-browsing rules, storage
          limits, and any device backup policy you use.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can delete individual documents or explicitly clear Open Mermaid’s local data.
          Clearing browser site data, using automated cleanup, or losing the browser profile can
          also remove it. Export important source to a file you control.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Optional local performance history</h2>
        <p>
          The performance page measures supported Web Vitals for the current visit in your browser.
          Those readings are ephemeral unless you explicitly enable 30-day local history. When
          enabled, a separate bounded IndexedDB database stores only a metric ID, metric name,
          numeric value and rating, fixed page category, navigation type, small-or-large viewport
          class, timestamp, and schema version.
        </p>
        <p>
          Local performance records do not contain diagram source or titles, raw paths, queries,
          fragments, referrers, user-agent strings, DOM nodes, selectors, resource URLs, or
          performance-entry objects. They are not transmitted. You can clear this history on the
          performance page or with the editor’s clear-local-data action.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Network requests</h2>
        <p>
          The browser requests the HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and other static assets needed to load the
          site. The hosting provider can receive ordinary request information such as IP address,
          user agent, requested path, time, and security logs under its own policy. Open Mermaid v1
          makes no network analytics, outbound telemetry, remote-font, or runtime CDN request. The
          optional production CrUX report runs in project automation and does not add a browser
          request.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Sharing with a fragment link</h2>
        <p>
          When you explicitly create a snapshot link, Open Mermaid compresses selected diagram data
          into the URL fragment after #. Browsers normally omit the fragment from the HTTP request.
          The fragment is not encrypted: recipients, browser history, clipboard tools, extensions,
          screenshots, screen sharing, and applications where you paste it may see the content.
        </p>
        <p>
          Snapshot links are immutable copies. Editing or deleting the local document does not
          revoke a link already sent to someone. Do not include secrets, credentials, regulated
          data, or confidential information unless your policies explicitly allow the sharing
          channel.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Exports, imports, and clipboard actions</h2>
        <p>
          Import reads the file you select in the browser. Export creates a source, SVG, or PNG file
          on your device. Copy actions place selected source or sanitized SVG on the system
          clipboard. After an explicit export, copy, upload, paste, or share action, the
          destination’s privacy and retention rules apply.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Future optional modes</h2>
        <p>
          ChatGPT, AI, cloud sync, and collaboration are not part of this standalone notice. If
          introduced as separately deployed optional capabilities, each must explain which
          user-selected content leaves the device, which service receives it, why, and how long it
          is retained before the user connects it.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Questions</h2>
        <p>
          A contact channel for privacy questions will be published here before production launch.
          Do not send private diagram content, credentials, vulnerability details, or other
          sensitive data through public channels.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .policy-copy {
    padding-block-end: 3rem;
  }

  .policy-copy section {
    padding-block: 0.4rem 1rem;
  }
</style>
